#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "c1dcb0a7-441f-5628-ba23-71d43d9ea96c" :question/math-academy-id "e-495" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int x^2\\cos(3x) \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int x\\sin(3x) \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{27}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2{C}_{1}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}((9{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{2C_1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "b81ff533-2067-5d1a-b265-df1ad91fa8da" :question/math-academy-id "e-496" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^e 32x^3(\\ln x)^2 \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=32{x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 32{x}^{3}\\text{d}x=8{x}^{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(8{x}^{4})-\\int (8{x}^{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int {x^3\\ln(x)} \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is an integral constant. So, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16(\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{4}-16{C}_{1} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -4C_1.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}32{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & ={{x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[(e)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1)]-[(1)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{4}(8-4+1)]-1 \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "8ec71af3-4534-5ef1-8745-3220102ba838" :question/math-academy-id "e-7053" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (t^2+1)e^{2t+1} \\, \\textrm{d} t.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int t\\,e^{2t+1}\\,\\textrm{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-(\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -{D}.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+3))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(2-2+3)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{3e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}-3e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab0674c-09c8-5240-aedf-c035249acffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(2{x}^{2}-2x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302473} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba89f8-5588-557a-86cc-689cccf65370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(2{x}^{2}+4x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302474} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7bd74f-53ad-5c3e-90fa-51e8ab8d8398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302475} {:answer/id #uuid "285f891a-bd61-57e3-a6a8-f017ae3233f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}({x}^{2}-2x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302476} {:answer/id #uuid "07e52578-78ff-5fd8-bdfb-4995a16fe528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-2x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7bd74f-53ad-5c3e-90fa-51e8ab8d8398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302475} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa2bca9-1d3d-58d9-86bb-02ea3d2d3255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a54a5649-a9ff-55b5-934d-9e2c6e06e573" :question/math-academy-id "q-114693" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2})(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{2x}(2x-1)-4D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-4x+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c005183d-66c2-596d-8ebf-f8fb107e87d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x({log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302480} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccced5a-a91e-592e-9d56-8121ee8fb5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x({({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302481} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe92e85-cb8c-5e90-a327-d1625366ba62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x({({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}+\\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302482} {:answer/id #uuid "7680a0e1-6003-53fb-a04d-ae82b5801d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x({log}_{4}⁡(x)+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302483} {:answer/id #uuid "e262f3e1-fc45-5e32-a420-b98d80d52360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}+2{log}_{4}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ccced5a-a91e-592e-9d56-8121ee8fb5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x({({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302481} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ddaba5-abc7-516d-b907-a4dbdb41bb07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "854f6c95-7a5c-5893-a4e7-acee80cffe1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114880" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡(x))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =x({({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccf920b0-c603-5b4b-9894-b5580bc029dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302487} {:answer/id #uuid "9227ac69-618d-52b7-b123-eaffeb27cf8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302488} {:answer/id #uuid "558130a4-ecce-5242-bcb3-0c26016995b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302489} {:answer/id #uuid "ff54e76f-ad97-5bc7-ae58-b3cd118e556b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302490} {:answer/id #uuid "37b7ff5e-7356-535f-82ee-572d6bc586a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9227ac69-618d-52b7-b123-eaffeb27cf8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302488} :answer-field/id #uuid "2081bf06-435f-58de-b4ce-ab2a250a926c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12877697-16da-579f-9576-412230e02539" :question/math-academy-id "q-114900" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡x)}^{2}(2{x}^{2})-\\int (2{x}^{2})(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡x)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$ \n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-2+1)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5dec534-e6c9-5070-8786-6f25b314c625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e^4-3e^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5dec534-e6c9-5070-8786-6f25b314c625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e^4-3e^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302494} :answer-field/id #uuid "c231abfc-17dd-5646-b07e-424cbb758afe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58183e98-112b-5d7f-a446-eff140deec46" :question/math-academy-id "q-351983" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{2t+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 3{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2})(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2})-\\int (\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2})(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2})-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2})(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3(\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-3D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t+2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}({1}^{2}){e}^{2(1)+2}-\\frac{3}{2}(1){e}^{2(1)+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2(1)+2})-(\\frac{3}{2}({0}^{2}){e}^{2(0)+2}-\\frac{3}{2}(0){e}^{2(0)+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2(0)+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{4}-3{e}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e552836-e876-5367-b359-666e55840706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11e^3-7e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e552836-e876-5367-b359-666e55840706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11e^3-7e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302497} :answer-field/id #uuid "465f49d9-5be2-5d73-8dfc-ef1e71528755" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c8b6c76-c8f1-56e8-9089-40d17974e721" :question/math-academy-id "q-351991" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={y}^{2}+3\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+3)(\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=y\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y)(\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-(\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+7)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+7)]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(2)-1}}{4}(2(2)^{2}-2(2)+7))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)-1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+7)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(8-4+7)-\\frac{e}{4}(2-2+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{7e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{3}-7e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758e634e-a84f-58f8-ad7b-19ef67456cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13e^5-9e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302500} {:answer/id #uuid "422aa673-2aa4-5d1d-a9ec-c23b58488460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "758e634e-a84f-58f8-ad7b-19ef67456cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13e^5-9e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302500} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ca39a9-3d1d-50fc-8834-0a2e97c1a244" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3372f31-2811-525d-bb58-ef3ae85f616b" :question/math-academy-id "q-351994" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={y}^{2}+4\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+4)(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=y\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y)(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-(\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+9)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+9)]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(2)+1}}{4}(2(2)^{2}-2(2)+9))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+9)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5}}{4}(8-4+9)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{5}}{4}-\\frac{9e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{5}-9e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669a636b-6611-50b8-84df-2a22afa2af9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669a636b-6611-50b8-84df-2a22afa2af9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302504} :answer-field/id #uuid "1747833e-00a1-5a08-8b51-d14818791771" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a32abdd-07d2-5cd6-a901-13295b56bc62" :question/math-academy-id "q-352011" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+2\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =((4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+2+0-8)-0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7e3cbc-ab0b-584d-9f78-750a8e82180d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302507} {:answer/id #uuid "47dc2ddb-39d8-56b5-b5f5-66320dd2fca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a7e3cbc-ab0b-584d-9f78-750a8e82180d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302507} :answer-field/id #uuid "1558dd5c-dd70-57ad-adab-2b2fbc884a12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7fb7e80-d19f-5ebe-bec3-1c2b35c237a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352026" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2(-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}+D) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\frac{2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{3}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{9} \\\\  & \\,-((0)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\frac{2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{3}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+0+\\frac{2}{9}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8-9{\\pi }^{2}}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-9{\\pi }^{2}}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e51810-23e4-5e52-935a-3629411dd774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23e51810-23e4-5e52-935a-3629411dd774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302511} :answer-field/id #uuid "28351b5f-e013-5b3b-aaa4-4ba297ba17fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da8f5fbe-dbfb-5847-a76c-cf802c186be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-352040" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3 & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x=3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}(3x)-\\int (3x)(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6 & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)(6x)-\\int (6x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-6x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-6x+D) \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+6x-D \\\\  & =3x((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[3x((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)]}_{1/3}^{e/3} \\\\  & =[3(\\frac{e}{3})({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3}))}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3})+2)]-[3(\\frac{1}{3})({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}))}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+2)] \\\\  & =[e((\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[1((\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[e(1-2+2)]-[1(0-0+2)] \\\\  & =e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aa41091-0ab2-5f4e-ad39-e11086965817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^3-2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290226} {:answer/id #uuid "ea22a52c-d32e-5dbb-9616-c24a9f1e2545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aa41091-0ab2-5f4e-ad39-e11086965817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^3-2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290226} :answer-field/id #uuid "2110f93c-9dc8-5850-8e88-a44f5f8650af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1ddafc6-2561-5d34-8429-bede58c8f919" :question/math-academy-id "q-352049" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}243{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=243{x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 243{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=81{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 243{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}^{2}(81{x}^{3})-\\int (81{x}^{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =81{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}^{2}-162\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 243{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =81{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}^{2}-162\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =81{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}^{2}-162(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =81{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}^{2}-54{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+18{x}^{3}-162D \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+18)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-162D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}243{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{3}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+18)]}_{1/3}^{e/3} \\\\  & =[{(\\frac{e}{3})}^{3}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3}))}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3})+18)] \\\\  & \\,-[{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}))}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+18)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{27}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+18)]-[\\frac{1}{27}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+18)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{27}(81-54+18)]-\\frac{1}{27}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a1358b0-9729-5d6a-a201-ef678d43faa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^3-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a1358b0-9729-5d6a-a201-ef678d43faa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^3-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302515} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4745872-a67c-5b61-8345-e753e8b92087" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a00e4cd0-24d1-56ef-8808-550833053576" :question/math-academy-id "q-352056" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=27(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 27(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=9(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}(9(x+1)^{3})-\\int (9(x+1)^{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(x+1)^{3}-18D \\\\  & =(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-18D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)]}_{0}^{e-1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[{1}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9-6+2)]-2 \\\\  & =5{e}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "844c286d-0c7b-5ffb-b04c-5470df58d20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13e^2-13}{4e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302518} {:answer/id #uuid "9c60044b-0e85-5729-a360-0f367e77cf86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "844c286d-0c7b-5ffb-b04c-5470df58d20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13e^2-13}{4e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302518} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eda6192-8fa5-57f7-9ded-e1ca0c59afb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2db99988-2dc4-5339-bcd9-5947f44596b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-352111" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+6\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6)(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-(\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+13)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+13)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)-1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+13))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)-1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+13)) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{4}(2-2+13)-\\frac{1}{4e}(0-0+13) \\\\  & =\\frac{13e}{4}-\\frac{13}{4e} \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{2}-13}{4e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c0bcc6-a786-5720-bdac-aee2e5a413fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02c0bcc6-a786-5720-bdac-aee2e5a413fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302522} :answer-field/id #uuid "920b4380-bbfc-5f6f-8d45-af7d11b9adfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8a660ee4-8847-5572-93f4-6c44163c7159" :question/math-academy-id "q-352117" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{t}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=8t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(8t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={[-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(0+8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1)+0)-(0+0+8) \\\\  & =4\\pi -8 \\\\  & =4\\pi -8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a9b071-3eb0-5171-9e71-fb71052b1031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290237} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5b3f94-5e85-5322-abb5-034e8f7d8372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290238} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ec805b-820f-55b0-a01e-d13461e7dccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290239} {:answer/id #uuid "d117ffe3-c3d7-5eee-ae31-b5b703cd18d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290240} {:answer/id #uuid "b0547144-d893-58da-af99-1ef6b0308476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00a9b071-3eb0-5171-9e71-fb71052b1031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290237} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ea7374-e58c-515b-af1a-7c1970f023c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f83185ca-7204-529d-8697-1b2d513b1e26" :question/math-academy-id "q-50222" :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239ca984-0673-5dc3-869a-a69a172bdc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302525} {:answer/id #uuid "8295bea8-f0f8-5ddf-b84b-eac7a5ca4f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302526} {:answer/id #uuid "c679975a-a8ce-5ce4-8d62-c1307c900703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302527} {:answer/id #uuid "67c1699b-1a77-5ce4-9cda-80948416d814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302528} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe86d56-49dc-5043-aaee-5ff593ce6717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c679975a-a8ce-5ce4-8d62-c1307c900703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302527} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c3c93a-5ad7-5e7a-98f3-94a316b5da1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e9d38a9-3f2c-5ef9-8cf7-45c3113871ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-50226" :question/problem "$\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
